%I A398696 #20 Aug 13 2026 21:39:30
%S A398696 0,8,90,998,10980,120788,1328670,14615378,160769160,1768460768,
%T A398696 19453068450,213983752958,2353821282540,25892034107948,
%U A398696 284812375187430,3132936127061738,34462297397679120,379085271374470328,4169937985119173610,45869317836310909718
%N A398696 a(n) = floor(3*11^n/4).
%C A398696 For n >= 1, the base-11 representation of a(n) consists of the first n digits of the base-11 expansion of 3/4 = 0.828282..._11, i.e., n alternating digits 8,2,8,2,... beginning with 8.
%C A398696 The base-11 digits of a(n) and A398697(n) sum to 10 in every position.
%C A398696 Base-11 analog of A397936 (base 7); see A397936 for the classification of the pair floor(3*b^n/4), floor(b^n/4) over odd b by b mod 4.
%C A398696 For odd b == 3 (mod 4) the base-b expansions of 1/4 and 3/4 alternate the same two digits (b-3)/4 and (3*b-1)/4, leading with (b-3)/4 and (3*b-1)/4 respectively; these are the corresponding digits summing to b-1 (A397936). Since b-1 is even the two digits have equal parity, and for prime b, binomial(b-1,k) == (-1)^k (mod b), so Lucas' theorem gives binomial(b^n - 1, floor(3*b^n/4)) == (-1)^(n*(b-3)/4) (mod b) for n >= 1.
%C A398696 Therefore, for prime b, binomial(b^n - 1, floor(3*b^n/4)) == 1 (mod b) when b == 3 (mod 8) and == (-1)^n (mod b) when b == 7 (mod 8), splitting the b == 3 (mod 4) case of A397936 by b mod 8. Base 7 gives the (-1)^n case; base 3 gives the identically-1 case but the pair coincides up to offset (see A397936), so base 11 is the smallest b == 3 (mod 8) for which floor(b^n/4) and floor(3*b^n/4) are distinct sequences.
%H A398696 Reed L. Rucka, <a href="/A398696/b398696.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..950</a>
%H A398696 <a href="/index/Rec#order_03">Index entries for linear recurrences with constant coefficients</a>, signature (11,1,-11).
%F A398696 a(n) = (3*11^n - (-1)^n - 2)/4.
%F A398696 a(n) = 11*a(n-1) + 5 - 3*(-1)^n for n >= 1, a(0) = 0.
%F A398696 a(n) = 11*a(n-1) + a(n-2) - 11*a(n-3) for n >= 3.
%F A398696 a(n) + A398697(n) = A024127(n).
%F A398696 a(n) + a(n+1) = A199029(n).
%F A398696 binomial(11^n - 1, a(n)) == 1 (mod 11) for n >= 1 (Lucas: 11^n - 1 is all 10's in base 11 and the base-11 digits of a(n) are all 8 or 2, with binomial(10,8) = binomial(10,2) = 45 == 1 (mod 11), so every position contributes a factor 1).
%F A398696 Limit_{n->oo} a(n)/11^n = 3/4.
%F A398696 G.f.: 2*x*(4 + x)/((1 - x)*(1 + x)*(1 - 11*x)).
%F A398696 E.g.f.: (3*exp(11*x) - 2*exp(x) - exp(-x))/4.
%e A398696 a(3) = 998 = 828 in base 11; a(4) = 10980 = 8282 in base 11.
%p A398696 seq(floor(3*11^n/4), n=0..20);
%t A398696 Table[Floor[3*11^n/4], {n, 0, 20}]
%o A398696 (PARI) a(n) = 3*11^n\4;
%Y A398696 Cf. A397936 (base-7 analog), A125833 (base-5 analog), A081251 (base-3 analog), A001020 (powers of 11), A398697 (complement within 11^n - 1), A024127 (11^n - 1), A199029 (consecutive-term sums).
%K A398696 nonn,easy,base
%O A398696 0,2
%A A398696 _Reed L. Rucka_, Aug 07 2026
